note: or_else runs a block when the statement before it fails with an error that can be caught.

set number a to 10
set number b to 0
set number result to a / b or_else do:
    print("division by zero; using -1")
    change result to -1
end
print(f"result = {result}")

note: A position that does not exist is an error too.
set list items to ["a", "b", "c"]
set str safe_item to items[99] or_else do:
    change safe_item to "(none)"
end
print(f"safe_item = {safe_item}")

note: find finding nothing is not an error: it gives empty, so or_else does not run.
set str config to find("no digits here", p"[num]+")
if config is empty do:
    change config to "default_mode"
end
print(f"config = {config}")

note: or_else guards one statement. Use `as` to get the error as an object.
set number n to to_number("abc") or_else as err do:
    print(err.code)
    print(err.message)
    change n to 0
end
print(n)
